# Commit and Push After Code Changes -- User Runs Git, Not the Agent

Railway auto-deploys straight from `main`, and the user wants every code
change committed and pushed promptly -- but the user explicitly does NOT
want the agent invoking `git commit`/`git push` itself (even though it's
technically low-cost to do so). Every commit/push must be run by the user,
from their own terminal.

After making a code change in this repo (backend or frontend) and verifying
it (tests/build/lint as applicable), do NOT call the Shell tool with
`git commit` or `git push`. Instead, output a ready-to-copy command block in
the same turn -- don't wait to be asked:

```bash
git add <files>
git commit -m "$(cat <<'EOF'
<short summary of the change>

<optional longer explanation -- why, not just what>
EOF
)"
git push
```

- List the exact files to stage (avoid `git add -A` if there are unrelated
  stray/generated files sitting in the tree).
- `git status`/`git diff`/`git log` (read-only) are fine to run directly --
  only the actual commit/push must be left to the user.
- If the user explicitly asks the agent to run git itself for a specific
  case, that's fine for that instance, but default back to handing over
  commands afterward unless told otherwise.
